# Build context exclusions.
#
# Two distinct jobs, both load-bearing:
#
# 1. Keep documentation churn out of the context. The context digest is what BuildKit
#    compares to decide whether a stage is still valid, so a README edit that reaches the
#    context can invalidate the multi-hour OpenCV CUDA compile (FR-019).
# 2. Keep downloaded model binaries out of the context. Several hundred MB of .pt/.weights
#    may exist locally from computing checksums (see models/checksums.sha256); the build
#    fetches and verifies its own copies, so uploading these would add minutes to every
#    `docker build` for nothing.
#
# NOTE: `scripts/` and `models/` must NOT be excluded. The Dockerfile COPYs
# scripts/assert-cuda-build.py, scripts/export-onnx.py, scripts/warmup.py,
# models/checksums.sha256, models/yolov4.cfg and models/coco.names as build inputs.
# scripts/warmup.py is the only one of those that also SHIPS, as /opt/warmup.py.

# --- Version control and tooling -------------------------------------------------
.git/
.gitignore
.github/

# --- Specification and agent tooling ---------------------------------------------
specs/
.specify/
.claude/

# --- Documentation ---------------------------------------------------------------
# `**/*.md` rather than `*.md`: the bare form matches only the context root, so
# `models/README.md` would still be sent.
**/*.md
LICENSE

# --- Compose / local run files ---------------------------------------------------
docker-compose.yml
docker-compose.*.yml

# --- Downloaded model binaries (fetched and verified inside the build instead) ----
**/*.pt
**/*.onnx
**/*.weights
sample.*

# --- Local scratch ---------------------------------------------------------------
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
.venv/
venv/
*.log
*.tmp
*.swp
.DS_Store
